{"text_id": "text_7ca2f6d67cdf9184883e", "record_id": "article_7ca2f6d67cdf9184", "canonical_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/25112025/n-c-regulators-say-trumps-proposed-repeal-of-the-endangerment-finding-would-increase-criteria-air-pollutants", "publisher_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/25112025/n-c-regulators-say-trumps-proposed-repeal-of-the-endangerment-finding-would-increase-criteria-air-pollutants", "title_original": "N.C. Regulators Say Trump’s Proposed Repeal of the Endangerment Finding Would Increase ‘Criteria’ Air Pollutants", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Inside Climate News", "source_id": "INT004", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-11-25T23:33:51+00:00", "source_text": "There was a time when the air pollution in Charlotte, North Carolina, was so bad that the 871-foot Bank of America Corporate Center, the city’s tallest building, topped by an ornate metal crown, wore a veil of smog. It took years, but after North Carolina enacted more stringent regulations, the air in Charlotte, although far […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 330, "text_sha256": "13ea8066f2d3db1fcf684f39cb521a0ce930f5bae7f213655dc58b3c6e3ca854", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:49:36.966216+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [{"id": "standards", "label_zh": "标准与监管", "label_en": "Standards and regulation", "start": 279, "end": 289, "evidence": "rown, wore a veil of smog. It took years, but after North Carolina enacted more stringent regulations, the air in Charlotte, although far […]"}], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://insideclimatenews.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:49:36.987145+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-11-25T23:33:51", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.573678+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_6a909ac741bce0a1e36d", "record_id": "article_6a909ac741bce0a1", "canonical_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2025/11/this-college-is-turning-the-earth-into-a-giant-battery", "publisher_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2025/11/this-college-is-turning-the-earth-into-a-giant-battery", "title_original": "This college is turning the Earth into a giant battery", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Yale Climate Connections", "source_id": "INT008", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-11-20T12:00:00+00:00", "source_text": "Transcript: Swarthmore College in Pennsylvania will soon heat and cool its buildings by using the ground as a giant thermal battery. The college is building a central geoexchange plant connected to pipes that stretch 800 feet underground. In summer, the system pulls heat from buildings, transfers it to water in the pipes, and then pumps the warm water underground to store the heat in the earth. Drake: “In the winter, the system runs in the opposite direction. We extract heat from the ground, lift the temperature of that water using the geoexchange plant, and send that warm water to our campus buildings to heat them.” Elizabeth Drake is the assistant vice president for sustainability and strategic initiatives at Swarthmore.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 732, "text_sha256": "0a4290bdd5c5c2f32be0d441de597d785afefe4441b9b01482ade4e2dcbfd160", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:49.804996+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [{"id": "science", "label_zh": "气候科学与风险", "label_en": "Climate science and risk", "start": 506, "end": 517, "evidence": "ter, the system runs in the opposite direction. We extract heat from the ground, lift the temperature of that water using the geoexchange plant, and send that warm water to our campus buildings to heat them.” Elizabeth Drake is the assistant"}], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:49.855163+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-11-20T12:00:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.573678+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_c71413fb72474ba4a50a", "record_id": "article_c71413fb72474ba4", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/11/with-cop30-indigenous-brazilians-strive-for-new-resources-to-protect-nature", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/11/with-cop30-indigenous-brazilians-strive-for-new-resources-to-protect-nature", "title_original": "With COP30, Indigenous Brazilians strive for new resources to protect nature", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-11-20T09:52:34+00:00", "source_text": "For many Brazilians, the country’s Indigenous peoples are considered the main protectors of nature. This is one of the key findings of a new Greenpeace survey published in October: according to the study, when it comes to caring for the forests, 80% of respondents trust Indigenous groups over any other national institution. Yet the global […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 344, "text_sha256": "0744977734f5992aabbcbcf32c32b99c6aefdd5f7a8dd816c915957dbebe3b87", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:50:10.887539+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [{"value_text": "80%", "start": 246, "end": 249, "evidence": " This is one of the key findings of a new Greenpeace survey published in October: according to the study, when it comes to caring for the forests, 80% of respondents trust Indigenous groups over any other national institution.", "interpretation": "not_classified"}], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_