{"text_id": "text_4ac07097e5b5e33fa320", "record_id": "article_4ac07097e5b5e33f", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/08/indonesias-fish-farm-expansion-to-absorb-d-c-sized-swath-of-protected-forests", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/08/indonesias-fish-farm-expansion-to-absorb-d-c-sized-swath-of-protected-forests", "title_original": "Indonesia’s fish farm expansion to absorb D.C.-sized swath of protected forests", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-08-29T15:34:24+00:00", "source_text": "WEST JAVA, Indonesia – A billion-dollar push to expand fish farming on the island of Java to support Indonesian President Prabowo Subianto’s free school meals program will absorb protected forests nearly the size of Washington, D.C., according to the country’s largest environmental group. “This area would become one of the largest releases of protected forests […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 366, "text_sha256": "cacf5b8eafc0074b47e5d2c629b6b789a6c349290f1144314e9442442b350c61", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:51:12.997611+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "ID", "alpha3": "IDN", "name_zh": "印度尼西亚", "name_en": "Indonesia"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "ID", "alpha3": "IDN", "name_zh": "印度尼西亚", "name_en": "Indonesia"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:51:13.011964+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-08-29T15:34:24", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "ID", "alpha3": "IDN", "name_zh": "印度尼西亚", "name_en": "Indonesia", "start": 0, "end": 9, "matched_text": "Indonesia", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "title_original"}, {"alpha2": "ID", "alpha3": "IDN", "name_zh": "印度尼西亚", "name_en": "Indonesia", "start": 11, "end": 20, "matched_text": "Indonesia", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.573678+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_63617fb176957333188d", "record_id": "article_63617fb176957333", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/08/as-forest-elephants-plummet-ebony-trees-decline-in-central-africas-rainforests", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2025/08/as-forest-elephants-plummet-ebony-trees-decline-in-central-africas-rainforests", "title_original": "As forest elephants plummet, ebony trees decline in Central Africa’s rainforests", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-08-29T13:05:04+00:00", "source_text": "In 2017, when Vincent Deblauwe joined the Cameroon-based Congo Basin Institute (CBI) to study African ebony (Diospyros crassiflora) — economically valuable pitch-black, dense wood — the Indigenous Baka people accompanied him on his field trips. As they sat around campfires and trekked through the rainforests, Deblauwe tapped into their knowledge of flora and fauna, especially […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 382, "text_sha256": "d1f0e42b6158176f2220e31595553ab8dd4a15f08bfca0f865ccf012547de7e4", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:51:18.139545+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "CM", "alpha3": "CMR", "name_zh": "喀麦隆", "name_en": "Cameroon"}], "governance_themes": [{"id": "justice", "label_zh": "公正转型", "label_en": "Just transition", "start": 186, "end": 196, "evidence": "rican ebony (Diospyros crassiflora) — economically valuable pitch-black, dense wood — the Indigenous Baka people accompanied him on his field trips. As they sat around campfires and trekked through the rainforests, Deblauwe tapped into thei"}], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "CM", "alpha3": "CMR", "name_zh": "喀麦隆", "name_en": "Cameroon"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:51:18.174117+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-08-29T13:05:04", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "CM", "alpha3": "CMR", "name_zh": "喀麦隆", "name_en": "Cameroon", "start": 42, "end": 50, "matched_text": "Cameroon", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.573678+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_a978cc335b0565c1c24a", "record_id": "article_a978cc335b0565c1", "canonical_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2025/08/evacuate-now-what-it-was-like-to-sound-the-alarm-ahead-of-hurricane-katrina", "publisher_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2025/08/evacuate-now-what-it-was-like-to-sound-the-alarm-ahead-of-hurricane-katrina", "title_original": "‘Evacuate NOW!’: What it was like to sound the alarm ahead of Hurricane Katrina", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Yale Climate Connections", "source_id": "INT008", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-08-28T14:22:04+00:00", "source_text": "I first started blogging for Weather Underground, the weather service I founded, in the spring of 2005. For the first few months, it was a slow time for interesting weather events, and I had trouble finding topics to write about. I was relieved when June 2005 brought two Atlantic tropical storms to discuss. But as July brought an unprecedented five named storms, three hurricanes, and two major hurricanes that were the strongest hurricanes ever recorded so early in the season, I was ready for less to write about. The peak of hurricane season was still a month away.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 570, "text_sha256": "e5f9fb8a0ce808b2bf540980ae6ca283798df3955540201d323e5881951556a9", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:47:20.659426+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:47:20.692857+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-08-28T14:22:04", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.573678+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_ee4989621ebd6814805c", "record_id": "article_ee4989621ebd6814", "canonical_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/forests/indigenous-communities-fear-impacts-of-cambodian-wind-projects", "publisher_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/forests/indigenous-communities-fear-impacts-of-cambodian-wind-projects", "title_original": "Indigenous communities fear impacts of Cambodian wind projects", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Dialogue Earth", "source_id": "INT009", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2025-08-28T11:54:01+00:00", "source_text": "When workers came to take down trees in the forest about a kilometre from her home in September 2024, Klerng Nary had questions. But the workers did their best to avoid locals from the Indigenous Bunong community, and even refused to name their employer when asked. For Nary and other Bunong people living in Laoka village, the forest, which is located outside Sen Monorom, the capital of Cambodia’s western Mondulkiri province, is a place of cultural significance. It is the place they go to memorialise their ancestors – a ‘spirit forest’. Eventually Nary heard from local authorities that the trees were cleared for a road and a new wind farm, part of the country’s plan to add 900 MW of wind power. “[They] didn’t ask people about the consequences,” she said.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 763, "text_sha256": "0d6dcd1ddea6a3288a9cdc6418e47e0895ce8c3060225c0a74be5bc47453ea60", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:18.767713+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [{"value_text": "900 MW", "start": 681, "end": 687, "evidence": " Eventually Nary heard from local authorities that the trees were cleared for a road and a new wind farm, part of the country’s plan to add 900 MW of wind power.", "interpretation": "not_classified"}], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "KH", "alpha3": "KHM", "name_zh": "柬埔寨", "name_en": "Cambodia"}], "governance_themes": [{"id": "energy", "label_zh": "能源与产业", "label_en": "Energy and industry", "start": 691, "end": 701, "evidence": " were cleared for a road and a new wind farm, part of the country’s plan to add 900 MW of wind power. “[They] didn’t ask people about the consequences,” she said."}, {"id": "justice", "label_zh": "公正转型", "label_en": "Just transition", "start": 185, "end": 195, "evidence": " 2024, Klerng Nary had questions. But the workers did their best to avoid locals from the Indigenous Bunong community, and even refused to name their employer when asked. For Nary and other Bunong people living in Laoka village, the forest,"}], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "KH", "alpha3": "KHM", "name_zh": "柬埔寨", "name_en": "Cambodia"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://dialogue.earth/en/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:18.818740+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2025-08-28T11:54:01", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "KH", "alpha3": "KHM", "name_zh": "柬埔寨", "name_en": "Cambodia", "start": 389, "end": 397, "matched_text": "Cambodia", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headlin