{"text_id": "text_52341772bbf14a86e41e", "record_id": "article_52341772bbf14a86", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/coffee-agroforestry-promises-a-path-to-eudr-compliance-but-challenges-remain", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/coffee-agroforestry-promises-a-path-to-eudr-compliance-but-challenges-remain", "title_original": "Coffee agroforestry promises a path to EUDR compliance, but challenges remain", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-26T12:00:31+00:00", "source_text": "Over the last two decades, about 130,000 hectares, or 3,210,000 acres, of forest have been lost annually for coffee cultivation. Pressure on areas suitable for coffee growing, usually forest-rich habitats, is estimated to increase, as coffee production is expected to triple by 2050 to meet growing demand. Part of that demand is represented by the […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 352, "text_sha256": "db0126a9aab35afde94775ff3e544ba3310aa30fcee7a622de1107e06e800051", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.104367+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.117080+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-26T12:00:31", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_00e9bf7ac9b617654006", "record_id": "article_00e9bf7ac9b61765", "canonical_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/26122024/pollution-concerns-persist-after-philadelphia-refinery-closure", "publisher_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/26122024/pollution-concerns-persist-after-philadelphia-refinery-closure", "title_original": "Five Years After Philadelphia Refinery’s Closure, Pollution Concerns Persist", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Inside Climate News", "source_id": "INT004", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-26T10:00:00+00:00", "source_text": "When the South Philadelphia refinery was permanently shuttered by a catastrophic explosion in 2019 after some 160 years in operation, many residents and environmentalists welcomed it as the long-overdue end of the city’s biggest single source of air pollution. Neighbors in so-called fenceline communities around the sprawling 1,300-acre site three miles south of City Hall […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 377, "text_sha256": "3b26a9a33d10385a9ecff509cd07f1b3d3b303faa77ac1d8f89b14b294c71866", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:12.552012+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://insideclimatenews.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:12.580083+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-26T10:00:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_0dfd1ba461eee87e4fb7", "record_id": "article_0dfd1ba461eee87e", "canonical_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/25122024/mobile-bay-alabama-oyster-population", "publisher_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/25122024/mobile-bay-alabama-oyster-population", "title_original": "In Mobile Bay, the Oysters’ Tale of Woe", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Inside Climate News", "source_id": "INT004", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-25T10:00:00+00:00", "source_text": "Andy Depaola looks out across Mobile Bay, where water laps against the once-hidden pilings of his neighbors’ piers. For centuries, the bay’s thriving oyster reefs created prime habitats for hundreds of fish, shrimp and crabs. The water-filtering creatures cleaned the waste, contributed to wave control and helped maintain the shoreline’s sprawling seagrasses while supporting carbon […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 387, "text_sha256": "b2b8485991a2a29026545ed5a991c363fc9d70f4506786354cdb8635498b8b09", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:12.705484+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://insideclimatenews.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:12.737661+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-25T10:00:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_ba6c56807f4473443774", "record_id": "article_ba6c56807f447344", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/the-10-indigenous-news-stories-that-marked-2024", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/the-10-indigenous-news-stories-that-marked-2024", "title_original": "The 10 Indigenous news stories that marked 2024", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-23T19:04:24+00:00", "source_text": "2024 was a big year for Indigenous lands worldwide, whether through land rights gains, land grabbing, restoring spiritual connections to lands, or analyzing how these lands support biodiversity conservation. Land and land use remained contentious as different groups fought over the use of Earth’s space—including Indigenous lands—whether it was agribusiness to feed growing populations, organized […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 401, "text_sha256": "df93d5ec7625c09a37e715b97094e79b2f7a1febc7dc008efdb119d3cfb66537", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.302553+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [{"id": "justice", "label_zh": "公正转型", "label_en": "Just transition", "start": 24, "end": 34, "evidence": "2024 was a big year for Indigenous lands worldwide, whether through land rights gains, land grabbing, restoring spiritual connections to lands, or analyzing how these lands s"}], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.315912+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-23T19:04:24", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_93a2f79f96e9c09a787c", "record_id": "article_93a2f79f96e9c09a", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/time-is-water-a-cross-border-indigenous-alliance-works-to-save-the-amazon", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/time-is-water-a-cross-border-indigenous-alliance-works-to-save-the-amazon", "title_original": "‘Time is water’: A cross-border Indigenous alliance works to save the Amazon", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-23T16:45:56+00:00", "source_text": "This story was produced in partnership with the Pulitzer Center Rainforest Reporting Grant. YURIMAGUAS, Alto Amazonas, Peru — The boat sets sail early in the morning. The plan is to travel down the Huallaga River, reach the Marañón, then head north and sail up the Santiago River toward the border with Ecuador. But after a […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 327, "text_sha256": "20b29776c2fe4f1360dac44149f4469d5fb8dabab22b8ea8aecdd14aaf3cf4d9", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.349272+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru"}, {"alpha2": "EC", "alpha3": "ECU", "name_zh": "厄瓜多尔", "name_en": "Ecuador"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [{"id": "investment", "label_zh": "公共投入与融资", "label_en": "Public investment and finance", "start": 85, "end": 90, "evidence": "This story was produced in partnership with the Pulitzer Center Rainforest Reporting Grant. YURIMAGUAS, Alto Amazonas, Peru — The boat sets sail early in the morning. The plan is to travel down the Huallaga River, reach the Marañó"}], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru"}, {"alpha2": "EC", "alpha3": "ECU", "name_zh": "厄瓜多尔", "name_en": "Ecuador"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.365741+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-23T16:45:56", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru", "start": 119, "end": 123, "matched_text": "Peru", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "EC", "alpha3": "ECU", "name_zh": "厄瓜多尔", "name_en": "Ecuador", "start": 303, "end": 310, "matched_text": "Ecuador", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_27b1db0d70fc08d18ecc", "record_id": "article_27b1db0d70fc08d1", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/the-fuel-that-moves-people-the-ecuadorian-case", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/the-fuel-that-moves-people-the-ecuadorian-case", "title_original": "The fuel that moves people: the Ecuadorian case", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-20T20:50:14+00:00", "source_text": "Throughout most of the twentieth century, the Ecuadorian authorities pursued a geopolitical strategy that reflected a long-held conviction that they were cheated out of large territories in the Western Amazon. Most of their claims were adjudicated in favor of Peru and they were on the losing side of border disputes in 1860, 1903 and 1941. […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 344, "text_sha256": "8a5b487861814f03767351aafa9e69e313f5d2862e304304aecc6113f446d337", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.484892+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.497270+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-20T20:50:14", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "PE", "alpha3": "PER", "name_zh": "秘鲁", "name_en": "Peru", "start": 260, "end": 264, "matched_text": "Peru", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_40ac09f9bed5e0572129", "record_id": "article_40ac09f9bed5e057", "canonical_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/ocean/reading-the-seas-our-top-ocean-books-of-2024", "publisher_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/ocean/reading-the-seas-our-top-ocean-books-of-2024", "title_original": "Reading the seas: Our top ocean books of 2024", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Dialogue Earth", "source_id": "INT009", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-20T15:49:52+00:00", "source_text": "Wild seas, high seas, mapping the bottom and determining where the top really is – ocean-focused books published this year cover an incredible range, both literally and metaphorically. Here are some of our favourite ocean reads from 2024. Tell us what we missed and should add to our reading lists on Facebook , Instagram , X/Twitter , LinkedIn and Bluesky . What the Wild Sea Can Be – Helen Scales A sea change has unfolded beneath the vast surface of the ocean over the last few hundred million years. In her latest book, marine biologist Helen Scales rewinds the clock to sketch out this shift, starting with the trilobites that swam, crawled and drifted in ancient seas.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 674, "text_sha256": "d7047fd94f4cfff35a4da6098ee4a04202142d816b931faa9756cd69530e8f02", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:42.012850+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://dialogue.earth/en/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:42.045121+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-20T15:49:52", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_84c041e875c1a2b049d3", "record_id": "article_84c041e875c1a2b0", "canonical_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/20122024/us-forest-service-hiring-freeze-impacts", "publisher_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/20122024/us-forest-service-hiring-freeze-impacts", "title_original": "US Forest Service Hiring Freeze Could Have Long-Term Impacts", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Inside Climate News", "source_id": "INT004", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-20T09:55:00+00:00", "source_text": "On Sept. 16, the United States Forest Service (USFS) announced a hiring freeze for the next fiscal year, meaning the agency won’t be able to hire any seasonal employees in 2025. The decision stems from budget shortfalls as appropriations from Congress fell about half a billion dollars short of the Forest Service’s request and impacts […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 339, "text_sha256": "f825da4330d6b1e5797d09674d2587ae5a5cda0819774dba7e13c4936893dcff", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:17.897220+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://insideclimatenews.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:17.925679+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-20T09:55:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States", "start": 0, "end": 2, "matched_text": "US", "method": "country-acronym-v2", "source_field": "title_original"}, {"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States", "start": 17, "end": 30, "matched_text": "United States", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_7fee95ce94423916f26e", "record_id": "article_7fee95ce94423916", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/new-frog-species-show-how-geology-shapes-amazons-biodiversity", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/new-frog-species-show-how-geology-shapes-amazons-biodiversity", "title_original": "New frog species show how geology shapes Amazon’s biodiversity", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-19T11:34:50+00:00", "source_text": "The frog’s loud croaking turned out to be a call to its own demise. The researchers walking along the steep muddy bank on a rainy November day in 2022 in the Imeri Range on the Brazil-Venezuela border were alerted by the unfamiliar sound. They found the frog sitting outside the opening to a tarantula’s burrow […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 314, "text_sha256": "dd26ead36862c85f7f5908bc4c94093784cbf6f2db7c896a9c46056ce36950d3", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.767389+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "VE", "alpha3": "VEN", "name_zh": "委内瑞拉玻利瓦尔共和国", "name_en": "Venezuela"}, {"alpha2": "BR", "alpha3": "BRA", "name_zh": "巴西", "name_en": "Brazil"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "VE", "alpha3": "VEN", "name_zh": "委内瑞拉玻利瓦尔共和国", "name_en": "Venezuela"}, {"alpha2": "BR", "alpha3": "BRA", "name_zh": "巴西", "name_en": "Brazil"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.779409+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-19T11:34:50", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "BR", "alpha3": "BRA", "name_zh": "巴西", "name_en": "Brazil", "start": 177, "end": 183, "matched_text": "Brazil", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "VE", "alpha3": "VEN", "name_zh": "委内瑞拉玻利瓦尔共和国", "name_en": "Venezuela", "start": 184, "end": 193, "matched_text": "Venezuela", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_53c2828f86da2ac78045", "record_id": "article_53c2828f86da2ac7", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/even-for-progressive-danone-complying-with-eudr-is-a-challenge", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/even-for-progressive-danone-complying-with-eudr-is-a-challenge", "title_original": "Even for ‘progressive’ Danone, complying with EUDR is a challenge", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-19T11:07:06+00:00", "source_text": "French dairy giant Danone has been around for more than a century and operates across more than 55 countries, producing everything from yogurt and milk to protein drinks and baby formula. It did $28.9 billion in sales last year, maintaining its spot as the world’s top dairy producer. But to make all that happen, the […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 321, "text_sha256": "b0df507017f5583989b196eab287b7b24d559cdf961120dade7805041dc7e780", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.787168+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [{"value_text": "$28.9 billion", "start": 195, "end": 208, "evidence": " It did $28.9 billion in sales last year, maintaining its spot as the world’s top dairy producer.", "interpretation": "not_classified"}], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:35.801509+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-19T11:07:06", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_b2ee4462a5f0d29beea1", "record_id": "article_b2ee4462a5f0d29b", "canonical_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/19122024/maryland-ev-charging-federal-grants", "publisher_url": "https://insideclimatenews.org/news/19122024/maryland-ev-charging-federal-grants", "title_original": "Maryland Uses Millions in Federal Grants to Ramp Up EV Charging Network and Keep Up with Demand", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Inside Climate News", "source_id": "INT004", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-19T10:00:00+00:00", "source_text": "Maryland is making big moves to ensure that electric vehicle drivers across the state can hit the road without worrying about running out of juice. This week, the Maryland Department of Transportation (MDOT) issued a call for proposals to build up to 29 new EV charging stations across the state’s highways. The expansion is part […]", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 333, "text_sha256": "7660d7b03debbfdbf84591489b7e327c9a96526035f24151b0a5e826d4f0a21f", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:18.120994+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://insideclimatenews.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:18.149793+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-19T10:00:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_ae8f7560646a6324698d", "record_id": "article_ae8f7560646a6324", "canonical_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2024/12/bougie-moms-unite-our-superpowers-could-be-the-key-to-saving-our-kids-future", "publisher_url": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2024/12/bougie-moms-unite-our-superpowers-could-be-the-key-to-saving-our-kids-future", "title_original": "Bougie moms, unite! Our superpowers could be the key to saving our kids’ future", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Yale Climate Connections", "source_id": "INT008", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-17T12:00:00+00:00", "source_text": "I wasn’t born into bougie. Growing up, my family motto was more “make it work” than “make it luxe.” My dad, with his barbed humor, used to call me his beekhni baby. In Hindi, that means his “beggar baby.” I was the child he and my mom prayed to have over 14 long years and then clothed in borrowed mismatched clothing. I happily made do with patched-up hand-me-downs and my mom’s lopsided sewing skills. Back then, fun wasn’t something we paid for – it was something we found: riding bikes with the brakes half-broken or playing H-O-R-S-E with a strung-up laundry basket that had a hole cut out off the bottom. Fast forward a couple of decades and life looks very different. My husband and I have clawed our way to a more stable, comfortable existence.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 752, "text_sha256": "7ec5b5eb1f535d049b83a83649d40d494db14aefdec6055380b9233032aaec6e", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:48:40.762898+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:48:40.796723+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-17T12:00:00", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_ae52938bfee0db16ffbc", "record_id": "article_ae52938bfee0db16", "canonical_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/illegal-timber-from-amazon-carbon-credit-projects-reached-europe-u-s", "publisher_url": "https://news.mongabay.com/2024/12/illegal-timber-from-amazon-carbon-credit-projects-reached-europe-u-s", "title_original": "Illegal timber from Amazon carbon credit projects reached Europe, U.S.", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Mongabay", "source_id": "INT007", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-16T00:01:10+00:00", "source_text": "High-class pool decks, furniture, floors, ceilings and boats. Those are some of the primary destinations of the valuable tropical timber that leaves South America in large containers to supply markets in Europe and the U.S. But consumers may be taking home the spoils of crime. That may be the case for 21,538 cubic meters (760,607 cubic feet) of Brazilian timber exported to the U.S., Portugal, Belgium, Spain and France between 2023 and early 2024, according to an investigation by the Brazilian Federal Police. This timber originated in forest management plans owned by Ricardo Stoppe, a physician and entrepreneur who is suspected of leading a criminal group responsible for extracting 38,000 truckloads of illegal wood from the Amazon Rainforest.", "text_kind": "article_excerpt", "text_characters": 751, "text_sha256": "54502560f7848095cf77ffd9d7ba9d4ecd883ede6a871e7f43eec427e8016a1f", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_content_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:41.088932+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [{"alpha2": "PT", "alpha3": "PRT", "name_zh": "葡萄牙", "name_en": "Portugal"}, {"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States"}, {"alpha2": "BE", "alpha3": "BEL", "name_zh": "比利时", "name_en": "Belgium"}, {"alpha2": "FR", "alpha3": "FRA", "name_zh": "法国", "name_en": "France"}, {"alpha2": "ES", "alpha3": "ESP", "name_zh": "西班牙", "name_en": "Spain"}], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [{"alpha2": "PT", "alpha3": "PRT", "name_zh": "葡萄牙", "name_en": "Portugal"}, {"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States"}, {"alpha2": "BE", "alpha3": "BEL", "name_zh": "比利时", "name_en": "Belgium"}, {"alpha2": "FR", "alpha3": "FRA", "name_zh": "法国", "name_en": "France"}, {"alpha2": "ES", "alpha3": "ESP", "name_zh": "西班牙", "name_en": "Spain"}], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://news.mongabay.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:53:41.125657+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-16T00:01:10", "sample_frame": "public_archives_20261102", "sampling_method": "deterministic_uniform_url_hash", "sampling_probability": 0.30234709214938144, "country_evidence": [{"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States", "start": 66, "end": 70, "matched_text": "U.S.", "method": "country-acronym-v2", "source_field": "title_original"}, {"alpha2": "US", "alpha3": "USA", "name_zh": "美国", "name_en": "United States", "start": 219, "end": 223, "matched_text": "U.S.", "method": "country-acronym-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "PT", "alpha3": "PRT", "name_zh": "葡萄牙", "name_en": "Portugal", "start": 386, "end": 394, "matched_text": "Portugal", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "BE", "alpha3": "BEL", "name_zh": "比利时", "name_en": "Belgium", "start": 396, "end": 403, "matched_text": "Belgium", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "ES", "alpha3": "ESP", "name_zh": "西班牙", "name_en": "Spain", "start": 405, "end": 410, "matched_text": "Spain", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}, {"alpha2": "FR", "alpha3": "FRA", "name_zh": "法国", "name_en": "France", "start": 415, "end": 421, "matched_text": "France", "method": "country-span-v2", "source_field": "source_text"}], "country_tag_version": "country-span-v2", "quarantine_reason": "stored_excerpt_and_headline_do_not_establish_climate_scope", "quarantined_at": "2026-11-02T02:09:04.575747+00:00"}
{"text_id": "text_006d2928a5d6c1b45d4e", "record_id": "article_006d2928a5d6c1b4", "canonical_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/pollution/empowering-indian-street-vendors-to-transition-from-single-use-plastics", "publisher_url": "https://dialogue.earth/en/pollution/empowering-indian-street-vendors-to-transition-from-single-use-plastics", "title_original": "Empowering Indian street vendors to transition from single-use plastics", "title_zh": null, "title_en": null, "summary_zh": null, "summary_en": null, "source_name": "Dialogue Earth", "source_id": "INT009", "source_type": "professional_climate_media", "language": "en", "published_at": "2024-12-13T17:42:14+00:00", "source_text": "Involving street vendors in the process to determine the appropriate enabling conditions would not only enhance policy design, but also support the development of simple and innovative solutions", "text_kind": "source_excerpt", "text_characters": 194, "text_sha256": "850af67371d58e245bfc5dd38a1e4ce0659497c99132122f4d0af226e02806d4", "text_basis": "public_wordpress_excerpt", "collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:42.433331+00:00", "extractor_version": "1.0.0", "document_type": "source_report", "document_type_basis": "document_genre_not_independently_established", "event_date": null, "event_date_precision": null, "source_updated_at": null, "policy_direction": null, "legal_effect": null, "summary_model": null, "quantitative_claims": [], "conference_ids": [], "agenda_item_id": null, "thematic_day_id": null, "rights_status": "source_attributed_excerpt_not_full_text", "country_codes": [], "governance_themes": [], "policy_tools": [], "policy_stages": [], "track": "action", "country_mentions": [], "annotation_method": "dictionary-evidence-v1", "review_status": "machine_extracted_not_human_verified", "archive_source_endpoint": "https://dialogue.earth/en/wp-json/wp/v2/posts", "archive_collected_at": "2026-11-02T01:46:42.443589+00:00", "published_at_raw": "2024-12-13T17:42:14", "sample_frame": "public_archiv